\par }\pard\plain \s22\nowidctlpar\adjustright \b\f15\fs20\cgrid {\b0\f1\cgrid0 
We do not recommend running Motocross Madness 2 on computers using multiple-monitor systems. If you do attempt to run Motocross Madness in this configuration, the list of available video cards that is displayed when you first start the g
ame may incorrectly report the name of your video cards.
\par 
\par If you are running Windows 95 or Windows 98 with a multiple-monitor system, and a 2D video card is the primary video card and a 3D video card
 is the secondary video card, you need to run Motocross Madness 2 using software rasterization as your 3D renderer because the game only recognizes the primary video card (2D cards fall below the minimum configuration requirements for this game). Choose S
oftware Rasterization in the Select a 3-D Renderer dialog box that is displayed when you start Motocross Madness 2. (If you do not see this dialog box when you start the game, confirm that the Show 3-D Renderer Selector at Startup checkbox is selected 
\endash on the Advanced Options screen\endash and then restart Motocross Madness 2.)
\par 
\par If you are running Window 2000 with a multiple-monitor system, and a 2D video card is the primary video card and a 3D video card is the secondary video card, it is possible to switch the tw
o cards, making the 3D card the primary video card. If you make the 3D video card the primary video card, you can then use it when running Motocross Madness 2.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b \hich\af1\dbch\af16\loch\f1 4.40 Integrated video cards/devices that are disabled may appear as available in the Select a 3\hich\af1\dbch\af16\loch\f1 
-D Renderer dialog box when you start Motocross Madness 2
